@*
    Views/Products/Create.cshtml:
    *****************************
    This Razor View corresponds to the Create() GET action inside ProductsController.

    MVC Convention:
    ***************
        - ProductsController
                >> Create() [GET]
                        >> Views/Products/Create.cshtml

    This page displays the form for entering information for a new Product.

    At this stage:
    - The form does not contain existing product data
    - The form does not save anything to the database
    - The POST action will be introduced later

    Link:  https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/mvc/views/working-with-forms
*@

@model InventoryManager.Models.Product // The view's model type is "Product"

@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Create Product";
}

<div class="text-center">
    <h1 class="display-4">Create a New Product</h1>
    @* 
    BS class "display-4": 
    *********************
    
    - Is a Bootstrap display heading utility class
    - This class is provided by Bootstrap. It is NOT an ASP.NET Core MVC Tag Helper or Razor feature
    - Bootstrap provides display classes for creating headings that are larger than the standard heading sizes
    - The "display-4" class applies Bootstrap's display-heading styling to this <h1> element
    - The "display-4" class changes the visual appearance of the heading.

    Link: https://getbootstrap.com/docs/5.3/content/typography/#display-headings
*@
</div>


<p>Enter the information below to create a new product.</p>
@*
    TAG HELPERS:
    ************
    Tag Helpers enable server-side code to participate in creating and rendering HTML elements in Razor files.

    In this form we are using several ASP.NET Core MVC Tag Helpers:
        > Form Tag Helper => <form asp-action="Create">
        > Label Tag Helper => <label asp-for="Name">
        > Input Tag Helper => <input asp-for="Name">
        > Textarea Tag Helper => <textarea asp-for="Description">
        > Validation Message Tag Helper => <span asp-validation-for="Name">
        > Validation Summary Tag Helper => <div asp-validation-summary="ModelOnly">

    1) The ASP.NET Core Tag Helper attribute:
        
            > asp-action="Create"
    
        "asp-action":
            - Specifies the controller action to which the form should be submitted
            - The Form Tag Helper uses this information to generate the form's HTML "action" attribute
            - The browser then submits an "HTTP request" to that URL
            - The MVC routing/action-selection system determines which action handles the request.

    In this example: 
        > asp-action="Create"

    corresponds to: 
        > ProductsController.Create()

    Because this view belongs to "ProductsController", we do not need to specify:
        > asp-controller="Products"

    when submitting to an action in the current controller.

    Therefore:
        > <form asp-action="Create" method="post">
        represents a form that submits to the Create action of the current controller.

    NOTE:
    *****
    "asp-action" is NOT the same thing as directly "calling" a C# method.

    It provides routing information to the Form Tag Helper 
    so that ASP.NET Core can generate the appropriate HTML "action" URL.

    The browser ultimately submits an HTTP request to that generated URL.


    2) The HTML attribute "method":
        > method="post"
       
        "method" is a standard HTML attribute of the <form> element.
        It specifies the HTTP method that the browser uses when submitting the form.
        
        > method="post"
          means that the browser sends the submitted form data using HTTP POST.

      NOTE:
      *****
      - GET and POST describe the HTTP request method.
      - The parameters of an action method describe the data that the particular action needs to receive.
        
      POST Request:
      method="post"
            >>> browser sends HTTP POST
                    >>> ASP.NET Core receives the request
                            >>> [HttpPost] action is selected
                                    >>> Create(Product product)
                                            >>> Model Binding populates Product
      GET Request:
      method="get"
            >>> browser sends HTTP GET
                    >>> ASP.NET Core receives the request
                            >>> [HttpGet] action is selected
                                    >>> Create()
                                            >>> Model Binding populates action parameters, if any
        
        > The GET action displays the form.
        > The POST action will receive the submitted Product data.

    Link: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/mvc/views/tag-helpers/intro
    Link: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/mvc/views/working-with-forms
    Link: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.aspnetcore.mvc.taghelpers
*@

<form asp-action="Create" method="post">
    <fieldset>
        <legend>Product Information</legend>

        @*
            Validation Summary:
            *******************
            The Validation Summary Tag Helper displays validation errors from ModelState.

            It means the summary can display errors that are not associated with a specific property.

            In this example: 
                > asp-validation-summary="ModelOnly"

            "ModelOnly" => specifies that the validation summary displays only model-level validation errors. 
            Model-level errors are errors that are not associated with a specific model property.

            For example, a validation error that applies to the Product as a whole rather than specifically to 
            Name, Price, Description, or Quantity can be displayed in the validation summary. 
    
            Property-level validation errors are displayed separately beside their corresponding form fields
            by using the Validation Message Tag Helper: 
                > <span asp-validation-for="Name" class="text-danger"></span> 
        
            The "asp-validation-summary" attribute supports the following values: 
                > ModelOnly => Displays only model-level validation errors. 
                > All => Displays both model-level and property-level validation errors.
                > None => Does not display validation errors in the validation summary.

            So:
            - Model-level errors => asp-validation-summary="ModelOnly"
            - Property-level errors => asp-validation-for="Name", asp-validation-for="Price", etc.

            The Validation Summary Tag Helper is applied to a <div> element.

            Link: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/mvc/models/validation
            Link: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/mvc/views/working-with-forms?view=aspnetcore-10.0#the-validation-summary-tag-helper
            Link: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/mvc/views/working-with-forms
        *@
        <div asp-validation-summary="ModelOnly" class="text-danger">
        </div>

        @* 
        Using the ASP.NET attribute "asp-for" in both elements "label" and "input"
        instead of using the normal HTML attributes "for" and "id":

        - The Label Tag Helper generates the appropriate HTML label and associates it with the input
        - The Input Tag Helper generates the appropriate HTML input and its name/id attributes
        
        However we can still use "id" attributes for sure if we needed in JS or CSS

        so asp-for ="Name" => Where does "Name" come from?
        Remember that "Name" is one of the "Product" class fields,
        so to access this field "Name" which is part of "Product.cs" file which is inside "Models" folder,
        we need to include it here in this view file,
        unlike C# code when we use the keyword "using",
        in Razor views, we use @model InventoryManager.Models.Product

        To summarize:
        *************
        asp-for="Name" connects this HTML element to the Name property of our Product model

        Link: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/mvc/views/working-with-forms
        *@

        @* 
        Bootstrap Classes:
        ******************
        - class ="mb-3"
        - class ="form-label"
        - class ="col-form-label"
        - class ="form-control"

        We added these two BS classes although they are NOT required,
        however adding them will for visual enhancements

        Using the BS class "mb-3":
            > m = margin
            > b = bottom
            > 3 = spacing level 3
        
        This applies a margin-bottom value to the element.

        In Bootstrap 5.3:
            > spacing level "3" corresponds to "1 rem" when using Bootstrap's default spacing scale.

        We use "mb-3" to create vertical separation between the individual form-field groups,
        making the form easier to read and visually organized.

        Link: https://getbootstrap.com/docs/5.3/utilities/spacing/

        Using "form-label" & "col-form-label":
        ****************************************
        - form-label => for normal/stacked form:
            > Used for the standard vertically stacked form layout.

        - col-form-label => for horizontal/grid form:
            > Used with Bootstrap's grid system for horizontal forms,
              where the label occupies a grid column alongside the form control.
        
        Our current form uses a vertically stacked layout, so we use:
            > class="form-label"

        If we later change the form to a horizontal/grid layout,
        "col-form-label" can be used with the appropriate "row" and "col-*" classes.

        Link: https://getbootstrap.com/docs/5.3/forms/layout/
        Link: https://getbootstrap.com/docs/4.1/migration/#forms-1
        Link: https://getbootstrap.com/docs/4.1/migration/#forms-1
        Link: https://getbootstrap.com/docs/4.1/components/forms/#readonly-plain-text
        Link: https://getbootstrap.com/docs/4.1/components/forms/#form-controls
        *@

        <div class="mb-3">
            @* 
                Using the LabelTagHelper, which uses the Product.Name model metadata 
                to generate the appropriate HTML label and for attribute. 

                Property-level validation errors are displayed separately
                beside their corresponding form fields by using:
                    > asp-validation-for="PropertyName"

                For example:
                    <span asp-validation-for="Name"></span>
            *@

            @* 1) Identifying the field: *@
            <label asp-for="Name" class="form-label"></label>

            @* 2) Creating/binding the input: *@
            <input asp-for="Name" class="form-control" />

            @* 3) Displaying validation errors for that property: *@
            <span asp-validation-for="Name" class="text-danger"></span>

            @* 
            The "asp-validation-for" attribute:
            ***********************************
            - It's an ASP.NET Core MVC Validation Message Tag Helper attribute.
            - It specifies the model property for which the validation message should be displayed.
            - In this example, "Name" refers to the "Name" property of the "Product" model.
            - Validation errors for the Name property are displayed inside this <span> element.

            The Tag Helper uses the validation information available in "ModelState"
            and the validation metadata associated with the model property.

            Link: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/mvc/models/validation?view=aspnetcore-10.0
            Link: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/mvc/views/working-with-forms?view=aspnetcore-10.0
            *@
        </div>

        @*
            PRICE FIELD:
            ************
            Price is a numeric property in our Product model.

            The Input Tag Helper uses the model property metadata when generating the HTML input

            We will introduce more detailed validation rules for numeric values as the project progresses
        *@

        <div class="mb-3">
            <label asp-for="Price" class="form-label"></label>

            <input asp-for="Price" class="form-control" />

            <span asp-validation-for="Price" class="text-danger"></span>
        </div>

        @*
            DESCRIPTION FIELD:
            ******************
            Because Description can contain multiple lines,
            we use a textarea rather than a single-line <input>.

            asp-for="Description" connects the textarea to Product.Description.

            The Textarea Tag Helper can also generate the appropriate "id" and "name" attributes
            and use model validation metadata
        *@

        <div class="mb-3">
            <label asp-for="Description" class="form-label"></label>
            <textarea asp-for="Description" class="form-control" rows="3"></textarea>
            <span asp-validation-for="Description" class="text-danger"></span>

            @* 
             NOTE ABOUT HTML5 VALIDATION ATTRIBUTES:
             ***************************************
             We do not necessarily need to manually duplicate validation rules in the Razor View,
             by adding these HTML attributes (conditions):
             - required
             - maxlength = "100"
 
             For example, if our Product model contains:
         
                [Required]
                [StringLength(100)]
                public string Name { get; set; }

             > the validation rules are defined in the model.

             ASP.NET Core MVC's validation system uses this model metadata.

             The Input Tag Helper can then generate the appropriate HTML5 "data-val-*" validation attributes:
             For example, validation metadata can result in generated attributes such as:

                        data-val="true"
                        data-val-required="..."
                        data-val-maxlength="..."
                        data-val-maxlength-max="100"

             This allows the validation rules to be defined centrally in the model 
             instead of duplicating the same rules in multiple views.

             NOTE:
             *****
             This does NOT mean that HTML5 attributes such as:
               - required
               - min
               - max
               - step
               - type

             are forbidden!
             They can still be appropriate when they describe the HTML control or its browser-level behavior.

             However, we should avoid unnecessarily duplicating the same business/data validation rules in both:
                1) the Model
                2) the Razor View

             The model should remain the central place for our application's validation rules.
        
             So the summarized idea:
             ***********************
             1) Define model validation rules in the model 
                when those rules describe the validity of the model's data.

            2) Let ASP.NET Core MVC's Tag Helpers use the model metadata
               to generate the appropriate form markup and validation attributes.

            3) Do not rely on client-side validation alone.
               Server-side validation remains necessary because the client cannot be trusted.

            This helps avoid unnecessarily duplicating the same validation rules in both the model and the Razor View.

             Link: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/mvc/models/validation
        *@
        </div>

        @*
            QUANTITY FIELD:
            ***************
            Quantity represents the number of products available in inventory.

            The Input Tag Helper connects this input to Product.Quantity.

            Because Quantity is a numeric .NET property,
            the Input Tag Helper can determine an appropriate HTML input type 
            from the model property's type.
        *@
        <div class="mb-3">
            <label asp-for="Quantity" class="form-label"></label>
            <input asp-for="Quantity" class="form-control" />
            <span asp-validation-for="Quantity" class="text-danger"></span>
        </div>

        @*
            SUBMIT BUTTON:
            **************
            type="submit" is a standard HTML attribute.

            It tells the browser that this button submits the surrounding <form>.

            The form specifies:
                > method="post"

            Therefore: 
                Clicking this button causes the browser to submit the form using the "HTTP POST" method

            Bootstrap classes:
            ******************
            - btn
            - btn-primary

            provide the Bootstrap styling for the button.

            Link: https://getbootstrap.com/docs/5.3/components/buttons/
        *@
        <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">
            Create Product
        </button>
    </fieldset>
</form>
@*
    ABOUT CLIENT-SIDE VALIDATION:
    *****************************

    Besides "server-side validation", ASP.NET Core MVC can also support "client-side validation".

    The Input and Validation Tag Helpers generate the validation-related HTML attributes and markup.

    Client-side validation requires the appropriate client-side validation JavaScript libraries.

    The default ASP.NET Core MVC project template 
    commonly provides a "_ValidationScriptsPartial" partial view
    containing the required client-side validation scripts.

     It can be included in this view using:

        @section Scripts {
            <partial name="_ValidationScriptsPartial" />
        }

     The layout must contain:

        @await RenderSectionAsync("Scripts", required: false)

    so that the "Scripts"" section from this view can be rendered by the layout.

    NOTE:
    *****
    Client-side validation is an additional user-experience feature.

    Server-side validation remains necessary 
    because the server must never trust data simply because it passed validation in the browser.
*@